# Unified Shelf Pipeline

This is the single top-level folder for the shelf pipeline. It connects the
project stages into one runtime:

1. Detect products on a shelf image.
2. Classify each product crop with the memory-bank product recognizer.
3. Detect price tags.
4. Extract prices and classify each tag as promotional or regular.
5. Align price tags with products.
6. Write an annotated image and two CSV/DataFrame outputs per source image.

## Project Structure

```text
artifacts/                     Runtime detector and recognition artifacts
input_images/                  Local images to process
outputs/                       Generated annotations, crops, and CSVs
run_pipeline.py                Simple command-line launcher
src/unified_shelf_pipeline/    Installable runtime package
  cli.py                       Command-line interface
  config.py                    Runtime configuration and artifact paths
  pipeline.py                  Pipeline orchestration
  models.py                    Shared runtime data models
  diagnostics.py               Environment and artifact checks
  image_processing/            Image I/O, object detection, and geometry
  shelf_analysis/              Product alignment and promotion classification
  price_extraction/            Price-tag text extraction and price parsing
  product_recognition/         Memory-bank product inference
  result_output/               CSV schemas, annotations, and result writers
tests/
  unit/                        Isolated business-logic tests
  integration/                 Multi-stage pipeline tests
  smoke/                       Packaging and artifact checks
tools/                         Standalone product-inference command
```

## Default Artifacts

The pipeline uses the bundled artifacts by default:

- Shelf detector: `artifacts/shelf_detector/shelf_server.pt`
- Price-tag detector: `artifacts/price_tag_detector/best.pt`
- Product memory bank: `artifacts/product_memory_bank/product_memory_bank_real_dataset.pkl`
- Product thresholds: `artifacts/product_thresholds/thresholds.csv`

## Installation

Run every command in this document from the `unified_shelf_pipeline/` folder.
Python 3.12 is recommended. Create and activate a fresh virtual environment in
PowerShell:

```powershell
py -3.12 -m venv .venv
.\.venv\Scripts\Activate.ps1
python -m pip install --upgrade pip
```

Install exactly one requirements file. Each file installs the runtime
dependencies and this project in editable mode. For the default PyTorch
distribution:

```powershell
python -m pip install -r .\requirements.txt
```

For an NVIDIA GPU, select the one file matching the required PyTorch CUDA wheel
build instead of installing `requirements.txt` separately:

```powershell
# CUDA 11.8
python -m pip install -r .\requirements-cuda118.txt

# CUDA 12.1
python -m pip install -r .\requirements-cuda121.txt

# CUDA 12.8
python -m pip install -r .\requirements-cuda128.txt
```

Each CUDA file contains the complete pipeline dependency set and installs the
same local project. The NVIDIA driver must support the selected CUDA wheel
build. Confirm the installed PyTorch build and GPU visibility:

```powershell
python -c "import torch; print(torch.__version__); print(torch.cuda.is_available())"
```

Check dependencies and artifact paths without loading models:

```powershell
python .\run_pipeline.py --check-env
```

## Run

Place images in `input_images/`. Supported formats are `.jpg`, `.jpeg`, `.png`,
`.bmp`, `.webp`, `.heic`, and `.heif`.

Process every supported image in the folder:

```powershell
python .\run_pipeline.py --input .\input_images
```

The command writes results to `outputs/`. It also accepts a single image path;
use `--help` to see artifact overrides and all optional settings.

```powershell
python .\run_pipeline.py --help
```

## Pipeline Components

The detector tables below report validation metrics embedded in the bundled
checkpoints. They are reference values rather than full-pipeline benchmarks;
the validation datasets and splits are not included in this repository.

### Product Detection

The single-class YOLO model in `artifacts/shelf_detector/shelf_server.pt`
detects product boxes. The default confidence threshold is `0.25`, followed by
class-agnostic non-maximum suppression at `0.25` IoU to remove duplicate boxes.
Use `--product-conf` to change confidence or `--no-product-nms` to disable the
additional overlap filtering.

| Precision | Recall | mAP50 | mAP50-95 |
| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| 0.92918 | 0.89416 | 0.94918 | 0.72743 |

### Product Classification

Each detected product crop is embedded with
`Qwen/Qwen3-VL-Embedding-2B`. A FAISS cosine-similarity index retrieves the five
nearest memory-bank images, scores are averaged by product label, and the best
label is checked against its threshold from `thresholds.csv`. Predictions below
the threshold are returned as `__Unknown__`; the best and second-best scores,
margin, threshold, and rejection reason remain available for auditing.

### Price-Tag Detection

The single-class YOLO model in `artifacts/price_tag_detector/best.pt` detects
price-tag boxes with a default confidence threshold of `0.25`. Change the
threshold with `--price-tag-conf` when tuning recall versus false detections.

| Precision | Recall | mAP50 | mAP50-95 |
| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| 0.99163 | 0.99063 | 0.99474 | 0.89670 |

### Price Number Extraction

PP-OCRv6 reads each detected tag using Portuguese/Latin recognition. By default,
crops are enlarged `3x`, OCR candidates below `0.40` confidence are ignored,
and up to three spatially ordered prices are returned. The parser joins split
whole/cents fragments, preserves price tiers, rejects quantity text such as
`600 g`, and retries difficult tags with a lower crop and contrast enhancement.
Use the `--ocr-*` options to tune it or `--no-ocr` to disable OCR.

### Promotion Classification

Promotion classification combines tag color, shelf-local size and shape, and
OCR keywords such as `OFERTA`. It uses surrounding tags to infer the regular
store palette and returns `uncertain` when the evidence is insufficient. Disable
it with `--no-sale-classification`; use `--sale-regular-tag-color` for a known
white, yellow, or red store format.

### Price-Tag/Product Alignment

Only products above a price tag are candidates. Tags are grouped into shelf
rows, products are assigned to the nearest row below them, and each row is
resolved as an ordered one-to-one match so connections remain shelf-local and
do not cross. Excess or implausibly distant detections can remain unmatched.

When the closest crop is unknown, a known product directly above the tag or a
strong same-row label consensus may correct the match. The CSV output records
the original candidate, selected product, distances, costs, correction reason,
and alignment status.

## Outputs

For each image, the pipeline writes:

```text
outputs/
  image_stem/
    annotated.jpg
    products.csv
    price_tags.csv
    product_crops/
    price_tag_crops/
  all_products.csv
  all_price_tags.csv
```

`products.csv` contains product boxes, classification scores and rejection
evidence, plus aligned price-tag information. `price_tags.csv` contains tag
boxes, up to three prices and OCR confidences, promotion evidence, and alignment
details. `all_products.csv` and `all_price_tags.csv` combine those rows across
all processed images. The same tables are returned in memory as pandas
DataFrames.

## Python API

```python
from unified_shelf_pipeline import ShelfPipeline, UnifiedPipelineConfig

pipeline = ShelfPipeline(UnifiedPipelineConfig())
results = pipeline.process("path/to/shelf_images")

for result in results:
    print(result.products_df)
    print(result.price_tags_df)
    print(result.annotated_image_path)
```

## Standalone Product Inference

Run product classification without shelf or price-tag detection:

```powershell
python .\tools\run_product_inference.py --help
```

Memory-bank creation, dataset splitting, embedding caching, and threshold
optimization are outside this repository; this tool consumes inference-ready
artifacts only.

## Tests

```powershell
python -B -m unittest discover -s tests
```
